          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0244",
              "exact_quote": "In java we have this scanner class that is defined in java.util package."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0245",
              "exact_quote": "Let's import this and create a scanner object, so new scanner."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0246",
              "exact_quote": "Now here inside this parenthesis, we need to specify where we're going to read data from."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0249",
              "exact_quote": "To work with the terminal window, we type system.in."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0252",
              "exact_quote": "So we have system.in, we also have system.out, which we use to print something on the terminal, right?"
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0253",
              "exact_quote": "Now let's use system.in and terminate this statement with a semicolon."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0254",
              "exact_quote": "Now this object has a bunch of methods for reading data, and all these methods start with next."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0255",
              "exact_quote": "There are methods nextByte, nextLine, nextBoolean for reading a byte, a line, and a boolean respectively."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0256",
              "exact_quote": "Calling the nextByte method returns a byte value that can be stored in a byte variable."
            }
          ],
